abstract: >-
  A personal essay collection and structured knowledge graph mapping the three
  major Buddhist Abhidharma traditions (Theravāda, Sarvāstivāda, Yogācāra) onto
  contemporary frameworks in computational phenomenology — predictive processing,
  active inference, Global Workspace Theory, Parallel Distributed Processing, and
  related approaches. Comprises eighteen essays (nine themes × English + Japanese), a
  cross-tradition dharma comparison table (~130 dharmas across nine sheets), and
  a JSON-LD knowledge graph (316 nodes, schema.org + custom vocabulary) with
  bilingual labels and Sanskrit / Pāli / Chinese philological alignment. An
  interactive 2D/3D viewer is provided. v0.5.0 adds Theme 9 (On embodiment in
  the age of AI) — questioning the logical structure of the claim that AI lacks
  embodiment (The Blind Spot, 2024): from the process-structural view of mind
  shared by Abhidharma, Yogācāra and emptiness thought, input-data provenance
  (organic vs. digital sensors) draws no essential line; what does the
  theoretical work is the feedback loop (autopoiesis; Friston 2018
  self-evidencing, structurally similar to Yogācāra's manas). Shows Buddhism
  itself splits on embodiment (only Theravāda posits the material heart-base
  hadaya-vatthu) and that defenders now stretch the concept (Froese 2026,
  distributed vs. organismic embodiment). First essay written as disclosed
  AI-mediated writing, carrying a machine-readable claims layer for LLM
  readers. Developed in collaboration with the Claude
  language model (Anthropic) for synthesis, cross-tradition mapping, and graph
  encoding; the author retains responsibility for all claims.
